This emits exactly the target's instructions and 58-insn count, except the break/loop-exit `j 0x...AC` block is placed BEFORE the `counter < max` test block; the ROM places it AFTER the test (target: bnez bit -> 0x9C(j exit), nop, j test(D0); test at 0x78; F4=0x9C j done; FC=0xA4 j loop). Mine: bnez bit -> 0x78(skip j exit), j test, 0x78 j exit, test at 0x80.

Semantics: do/for-ever loop: ++D_80300210; if (D_80300210 != D_80324E30 && !(D_80324B40[D_80300210] & 2)) continue; else if bit set exit loop; loop while D_80300210 < D_80324B94; return (D_80300210 < D_80324B94). D_80324B40 is a 0x14-stride struct array (s32 at +0), D_80300210/D_80324E30 are s32, D_80324B94 is s8. Also 'if (!(bit)) return 0' prologue variant seen in func_80051740. Tried: if/else, continue/break, && compound, explicit gotos, do-while vs for(;;) -- all give the same (or worse) block placement. Only remaining diff is gcc -O0 block ordering of the break-exit block; maybe original used a construct that defers the break block after the loop condition (both 0x80051658 and 0x80051740 share this exact layout).

Best score 375 (79%, 17 vs 18 insns). Best source (375):

typedef struct S86014Node { struct S86014Node *next; s32 key; struct S86014Node *unk8; } S86014Node;
void func_80086014(S86014Node *arg0, S86014Node *arg1) {
    S86014Node *cur; s32 key;
    cur = arg0;
    key = arg1->key;
    while (cur->next->key >= key) cur = cur->next;
    arg1->next = cur->next;
    cur->next = arg1;
    arg1->unk8 = arg0;
}
ROM semantics: insert arg1 (key at +4) into the linked list whose sentinel/head is arg0, before the first node with key >= arg1->key; finally arg1->unk8 = arg0.

What's left: ROM is 18 insns and starts `move t9,a0; lw t8,0(a0); lw t7,4(a1); lw t6,4(t8); slt at,t6,t7; bnez at,done; [loop] move t9,t8; lw t8,0(t8); lw t6,4(t8); slt at,t6,t7; beqz at,loop; done: lw t8,0(t9); sw t8,0(a1); sw a1,0(t9); jr ra; sw a0,8(a1)` - i.e. ROM uses prev(t9)/cur(t8) with a pre-test, $at for the slt, t regs throughout, and NO branch-likely. My output uses v0/v1/a2/a3, slt into v0, beql, and extra `lw` in the loop (17 insns). Tried: prev/cur version, cur/next version, register-qualified prev/cur/key - all 765/375, never the t-reg/$at shape. search_flags: all flag variants 375 except -O1 950. Maybe an older/different source shape (e.g. explicit if+goto, or pointer-to-pointer loop `SNode **pp`) produces the t-registers; worth trying `SNode **pp = &arg0->next; while ((*pp)->key < key) pp = &(*pp)->next;` style too.

